:title("Batch interpreter") ;
:description("Interprets Windows Batch (the cmd.exe .bat / .cmd command language) directly,
without generating code. The script is a flat list of lines - labels and statements - executed
by a program counter, so goto and call :label work; if/for bodies are parenthesized ( ) blocks
whose goto/exit signals propagate out to that program counter. Every value is a string, and
set /a arithmetic is 32 bit integer (an unset or non-numeric operand is 0).

Implemented: @ prefix and @echo off; echo text, echo. and echo(; set VAR=value, set VAR= (which
deletes), the quoted set form, set /a and set /p; the whole set /a operator set in the precedence
set /? documents (assignment and its compound forms, comma sequencing, | ^ and shifts, + - * /
and the modulo written %% in a file, unary ! ~ -, hex 0x and octal literals); %VAR% expansion
with the substring (:~n,m, n or m negative) and substitution (:from=to, case insensitive, and
the leading-* form that replaces everything up to and including the match) operators; delayed
expansion !VAR! under setlocal enabledelayedexpansion; setlocal / endlocal with a real
environment stack; %%i for-variables and %0..%9 / %* call arguments, both with the ~ modifiers
that are pure string operations (~ strips quotes, ~d ~p ~n ~x ~f and combinations split a path)
plus shift; if with string == (and /i), numeric EQU/NEQ/LSS/LEQ/GTR/GEQ, if defined, if exist,
if errorlevel, if not and an else branch; plain for, for /l counted loops and for /f over a
quoted string or the output of a command, with eol= skip= delims= and tokens= (including
n,m ranges and the * remainder); the command separators & && ||; redirection; labels :name,
goto name, goto :eof, call :name, call set (which expands a second time), exit /b [N], exit [N];
errorlevel as a pseudo-variable; rem and :: comments; the caret escape and the caret line
continuation.

Variable names are case insensitive, for-variable letters are not, and labels are case
sensitive. Keywords and switches (/a /f ...) are recognised in any letter case. %VAR% is
expanded the way cmd.exe expands it - the whole command line is expanded BEFORE it runs, so
%VAR% inside a ( ) block is the value from before the block while !VAR! is the current one.
The run's exit code is the script's, so a self checking script that ends with
`exit /b <failures>` is green exactly when it passes.

Two rules are worth spelling out because a straightforward reading gets them wrong. A ~
modifier run and a for-variable are both letters, so %%~ff and %%~pd are ambiguous; the rule
is that the LAST letter of the run is the variable, which is what the wine cmd suite under
tests/reference/batch expects. And parentheses are ordinary characters in a value, so
`echo Done (ok)` and `set x=a(b)` work - a run of them has to be BALANCED, which is what
keeps a value inside a ( ) block from swallowing the parenthesis that closes it.

There is NO filesystem behind a grammar run, so `if exist` answers from a small MODEL of the
paths every Windows installation has - the reserved device names (nul, con, prn, aux, com1-9,
lpt1-9) exist, C: and the standard Windows directories are directories, everything else is
absent. This is the same shape bash-interpreter.abnf uses for its -e -f -d predicates. The
model answers EXISTENCE, not contents and not a listing, and the rest follows from that: a
for /f file set always reads nothing (the loop runs zero times), for /r and for /d are not
offered at all because they enumerate a directory, the ~a ~t ~z modifiers answer empty
because they stat a file (~s asks for the short path, which is the long one when 8.3 names
are off, so it is a no-op flag and means the whole path when it stands alone), an output
redirection discards instead of writing, and stdin is end of file at once so set /p always
leaves its variable unchanged.

Also NOT modelled: external programs and pipes carrying their data; date/time, %RANDOM%,
codepages and the registry. A for /f command set is a chain of the internal commands that
produce capturable output - echo and ver.") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Hspace) ;

// ----- program: a flat list of labels and statement-lines (PC executes them) -----
Program     <~~ push(runProgram(takeAll())) ~~>
            = { Line } ;
Line        = BlankLine | ColonComment | LabelLine | StmtLine | StrayClose ;
// A ) that opens a top-level line closes no block: cmd.exe skips the line, which is
// what `call (` relies on - the lines after it run, and its ) is passed over.
StrayClose  = [ At ] ")" :whitespace() { NotNL } Newline ;
BlankLine   = Newline ;
ColonComment = "::" :whitespace() { NotNL } Newline ;
// An @ and blanks may stand before the colon, and blanks after it: `@  :  dest9`.
LabelLine   <~~ push({lbl: pop()}) ~~>
            = [ "@" ] ":" LabelName :whitespace() { NotNL } Newline ;
StmtLine    <~~ push({stmt: pop()}) ~~>
            = [ At ] Chain Newline ;
At          = "@" :whitespace() ;

// ----- command chains and redirection -----
// 'a & b' runs both, 'a && b' runs b only after success, 'a || b' only after failure.
// A redirection is attached to the piece it follows; there is no filesystem here, so an
// output redirection DISCARDS (the same capture machinery that backs for /f 'command').
// A trailing `&` with nothing after it runs nothing more (`call say&`).
Chain       <~~ push(makeChain(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Pipeline { ChainOp Pipeline } [ "&" ] ;
// `a | b` feeds a's output to b's input. Nothing here carries data between two
// commands (see the description), so a real pipeline is reported as not implemented.
Pipeline    <~~ push(makePipe(takeAll(), up.start)) ~~>
            = Piece { "|" NotBar Piece } ;
NotBar      = :script(~~
    (function() { return c.peek(0) === 124 ? abnf.newToken("\x01or-operator", 0) : undefined })()
~~) ;
ChainOp     <~~ push(up.in) ~~> = "&&" | "||" | "&" ;
// A redirection may also come FIRST: `> nul echo a` is `echo a > nul`. Every command
// of a chain may carry its own @ (`a&&@echo b`).
Piece       <~~ push(makeRedir(takeAll())) ~~>
            = { Redir } [ At ] Stmt { Redir } ;
Redir       <~~ push(mkRedir(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ RedirFd ] RedirOp RedirTarget ;
RedirFd     <~~ push({fd: up.in}) ~~> = "0"..."9" ;
RedirOp     <~~ push({op: up.in}) ~~> = ">>" | ">&" | ">" | "<&" | "<" ;
// Quoted and bare runs mix: `> "say one.bat"`, `>."\foo"`.
RedirTarget <~~ push({tgt: up.in}) ~~>
            = RTPart :whitespace() { RTPart } :whitespace(Hspace) ;
RTPart      = RTChar | '"' { NotDQNL } '"' ;

// A statement (also used inside ( ) blocks, where Newlines separate them).
Stmt        = Rem | EchoCtl | EchoDot | EchoParen | Echo
            | SetA | SetABad | SetP | SetQ | Set | SetDyn | SetShow
            | Setlocal | Endlocal | ShiftBad | Shift | Ver
            | If | ForL | ForF | ForDR | For
            | GotoEof | Goto | GotoDyn | CallSet | Call | CallExt | CallInt | ExitB | Exit | GroupElse | Group | ExtCmd ;

// Any other command - mkdir, cd, del, type, copy, a program name - is recognized with
// its argument text and reported as not implemented: there is no filesystem and no
// process table behind a grammar run (see the description). A statement keyword is
// never a command name here, in any letter case, so a keyword statement this grammar
// fails to read stays a parse error instead of turning into an unknown command.
ExtCmd      <~~ push(makeExtCmd(takeAll(), up.start)) ~~> = NotKwName ExtName LineText ;
// A %VAR% in the name is allowed: cmd.exe expands the line BEFORE it reads the
// command, so `%V%echo foo` is a command whose name only exists at run time.
ExtName     <~~ push(up.in) ~~> = ExtNamePt :whitespace() { ExtNamePt } :whitespace(Hspace) ;
ExtNamePt   = BareChar | "%" | "!" | '"' { NotDQNL } [ '"' ] ;
NotKwName   = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var w = ""
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc === 32 || cc === 9) { i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i) }
        var w0 = i
        while (i < w0 + 16 && ((cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122))) {
            w = w + String.fromCharCode(cc >= 65 && cc <= 90 ? cc + 32 : cc)
            i = i + 1
            cc = c.peek(i)
        }
        var kws = "|rem|echo|set|setlocal|endlocal|shift|ver|if|for|goto|call|exit|"
        var boundary = !((cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) || cc === 95)
        if (w.length > 0 && boundary && kws.indexOf("|" + w + "|") >= 0) {
            return abnf.newToken("\x01statement-keyword", 0)
        }
        return undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// ----- echo -----
Rem         <~~ push(makeNop()) ~~> = KwRem { NotNL } ;
// `echo on` / `echo off` toggle command echoing - but ONLY when the on/off is the whole
// rest of the line. Wine's expectation file records `echo off now` printing "off now"
// (test_builtins.cmd.exp line 58), i.e. it is an ordinary echo, so EchoEol asserts the
// end of the line before this rule may claim the statement.
EchoCtl     <~~ push(makeNop()) ~~> = KwEcho ( KwOn | KwOff ) EchoEol ;
EchoEol = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc == 32 || cc == 9) { i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i) }
        if (cc == -1 || cc == 10 || cc == 13) { return undefined }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01echo-eol", 0)
    })()
~~) ;
// `echo` glued to a "." ":" or "/" with NO space: the punctuation is a separator, not part
// of the text, and whatever follows it on the line IS the text. Ground truth is wine's
// expectation file (tests/reference/batch/wine-cmd-tests/tests/test_builtins.cmd.exp):
//     echo.       -> (empty line)      echo.word  -> word
//     echo:       -> (empty line)      echo:word  -> word
//     echo/       -> (empty line)      echo/word  -> word
// while the SPACED forms go through Echo and keep the punctuation:
//     echo .      -> .                 echo .word -> .word
// The :whitespace() comes BEFORE the separator: only the GLUED form is this rule.
EchoDot     <~~ push(makeEcho(pop())) ~~> = KwEchoW :whitespace() EchoSepCh LineText ;
EchoSepCh   = "." | ":" | "/" ;
// `echo(` prints an empty line and `echo(3` prints 3: the ( is the separator.
EchoParen   <~~ push(makeEcho(pop())) ~~> = KwEchoW :whitespace() "(" LineText ;
Echo        <~~ push(makeEcho(pop())) ~~> = KwEcho LineText ;

// ----- set VAR=value  /  set /a expr  /  set /p VAR=prompt -----
// set /a ignores double quotes wherever they stand - `set /a "x=1","y=2"` and
// `set /a ""x=1""` - so inside the expression a quote is whitespace (AWs).
SetA        <~~ push(makeSetA(pop())) ~~>
            = KwSet ( "/a" | "/A" ) :whitespace(AWs) Arith :whitespace(Hspace) { '"' } CmdEnd ;
// `set /a x=6 7` ("Missing operator") and other malformed expressions are an error
// cmd.exe reports with an errorlevel: reported as not implemented.
SetABad     <~~ takeAll(); push(batUnsup("a malformed set /a expression", up.start)) ~~>
            = KwSet ( "/a" | "/A" ) LineText ;
AWs         = { @+" \t" | '"' | LineCont } ;
// set /p prompts and reads a line; there is no stdin in this runtime, so it always reads
// nothing and (as cmd.exe does on end of file) leaves the variable unchanged.
SetP        <~~ push(makeSetP(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwSet ( "/p" | "/P" ) :whitespace(Hspace) SetName "=" ( LineText | Empty ) ;
// 'set "name=value"' is the quoted form: the quotes delimit the assignment rather than
// becoming part of the value, which is how a value with a trailing space is written.
// Text after the closing quote is ignored: `set "V=jim" fred` sets jim.
SetQ        <~~ push(makeSet(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwSet '"' :whitespace() SetName "=" ( QLineText | Empty ) '"' :whitespace(Hspace) [ SetQTail ] ;
SetQTail    <~~ pop() ~~> = LineText ;
QLineText   <~~ push(makeWord(takeAll())) ~~>
            = :whitespace() { QLinePart } :whitespace() ;
QLinePart   = ForRef | PctPct | VarPct | ParamStar | ParamRef | BangVar | PctLit | BangLit | QLineChars ;
QLineChars  <~~ push(makeStr(up.in)) ~~> = QLineChar :whitespace() { QLineChar } ;
QLineChar   = "\x09" | "\x20" | "\x23"..."\x24" | "\x26"..."\U0010ffff" ;   // not ! " or %
Set         <~~ push(makeSet(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwSet SetName "=" ( LineText | Empty ) ;
// `set %%i=` sets the variable a for-variable NAMES; the compiled half keeps one slot
// per name it can see, so a computed name is reported as not implemented.
SetDyn      <~~ takeAll(); push(batUnsup("set of a computed variable name", up.start)) ~~>
            = KwSet [ SetName ] DynNamePt :whitespace() { DynNamePt | SetNameCh } :whitespace(Hspace) "=" ( LineText | Empty ) ;
DynNamePt   = ForRef | VarPct | BangVar | ParamRef | PctLit ;
SetNameCh   = "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "0"..."9" | "_" | "." | "-" ;
// `set` / `set PREFIX` LISTS variables, with the names in the case they were written
// in, which this lower-cased environment no longer has; `set /Q`, `set =` and
// `set "="` are usage errors. All are reported as not implemented.
SetShow     <~~ takeAll(); push(batUnsup("set without a value", up.start)) ~~>
            = KwSet [ SetName | "/" :whitespace() SlChar :whitespace(Hspace) | "=" | '"="' ] CmdEnd ;
// The command ends here: only a redirection, a separator, a ) or the end of the line
// may follow.
CmdEnd      = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc == 32 || cc == 9) { i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i) }
        if (cc == -1 || cc == 10 || cc == 13 || cc == 62 || cc == 60 || cc == 38 || cc == 124 || cc == 41) {
            return undefined
        }
        var nx = c.peek(i + 1)
        if (cc >= 48 && cc <= 57 && (nx == 62 || nx == 60)) { return undefined }   // 2> 1<
        return abnf.newToken("\x01command-continues", 0)
    })()
~~) ;
// A name may carry . and - after its first character: `set WINE_expected.1=x`.
SetName     <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = ( "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" | "_" ) :whitespace() { SetNameCh } :whitespace() ;
Empty       <~~ push(makeStr("")) ~~> = "" ;

// ----- setlocal / endlocal / shift / ver -----
Setlocal    <~~ push(makeSetlocal(takeAll())) ~~> = KwSetlocal { SlOpt } ;
SlOpt       <~~ push(up.in) ~~> = SlChar :whitespace() { SlChar } :whitespace(Hspace) ;
SlChar      = "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" ;
Endlocal    <~~ push(makeEndlocal()) ~~> = KwEndlocal ;
Shift       <~~ push(makeShift(takeAll())) ~~> = KwShift [ ShiftOpt ] ;
// `shift /abc` and `shift /-1` are usage errors: reported as not implemented.
ShiftBad    <~~ takeAll(); push(batUnsup("shift with an invalid switch", up.start)) ~~>
            = KwShift "/" LineText ;
ShiftOpt    <~~ push(up.in) ~~> = "/" :whitespace() "0"..."9" :whitespace(Hspace) ;
Ver         <~~ push(makeVer()) ~~> = KwVer ;

// ----- goto / call / exit -----
GotoEof     <~~ push(makeGoto("eof")) ~~> = KwGoto ":" :whitespace() CiE CiO CiF :whitespace(Hspace) KwEnd ;
// 'goto :label' and 'goto label' are the same jump; ':eof' is matched by GotoEof above.
// A label ends at a second colon, and the rest is ignored: `goto :dest10:comment`.
Goto        <~~ push(makeGoto(pop())) ~~> = KwGoto [ ":" ] LabelName :whitespace(Hspace) [ GotoTail ] ;
GotoTail    = ":" :whitespace() { LineChar } :whitespace(Hspace) ;
// `goto :%next%` jumps to a label computed at run time; the compiled half lays its
// jumps out ahead of time, so this is reported as not implemented.
GotoDyn     <~~ takeAll(); push(batUnsup("goto a computed label", up.start)) ~~>
            = KwGoto [ ":" ] Word ;
// 'call set NAME=text' re-parses its argument, so the text goes through a SECOND round of
// %VAR% expansion - the documented way to expand a name that another variable holds.
CallSet     <~~ push(makeCallSet(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwCall KwSet SetName "=" ( LineText | Empty ) ;
Call        <~~ push(makeCall(takeAll())) ~~> = KwCall ":" LabelName { ArgWord } :whitespace(Hspace) ;
// `call other.bat args` runs another batch FILE - a command, like ExtCmd.
CallExt     <~~ push(makeExtCmd(takeAll(), up.start)) ~~> = KwCall NotKwName ExtName LineText ;
// `call echo %%f` runs an internal command after a SECOND expansion round, and a bare
// `call` sets errorlevel; only `call set` (CallSet) is modelled.
CallInt     <~~ takeAll(); push(batUnsup("call of an internal command", up.start)) ~~>
            = KwCall LineText ;
ExitB       <~~ push(makeExitB(takeAll())) ~~> = KwExit ( "/b" | "/B" ) :whitespace(Hspace) [ Word ] :whitespace(Hspace) ;
Exit        <~~ push(makeExit(takeAll())) ~~> = KwExit [ Word ] :whitespace(Hspace) ;

// ----- if / for (parenthesized bodies) -----
If          <~~ push(makeIf(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwIf { IfFlag } Cond Body [ KwElse Body ] ;
IfFlag      = IfNot | IfCi ;
IfNot       <~~ push({neg: true}) ~~> = KwNot ;
IfCi        <~~ push({ci: true}) ~~> = ( "/i" | "/I" ) :whitespace(Hspace) ;
// for /l counts start,step,end; for /f parses text; the plain for walks a word list.
// The three numbers are separated by a comma, a semicolon, an = or just whitespace:
// `for /l %%b in (1 1 3)` counts 1, 2, 3.
ForL        <~~ push(makeForL(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwFor ( "/l" | "/L" ) :whitespace(Hspace) ForVar KwIn "(" :whitespace(Hspace)
              ForNum [ ForLSep ] ForNum [ ForLSep ] ForNum
              ")" :whitespace(Hspace) KwDo Body ;
ForLSep     = ( "," | ";" | "=" ) :whitespace(Hspace) ;
ForF        <~~ push(makeForF(takeAll(), up.start)) ~~>
            = KwFor ( "/f" | "/F" ) :whitespace(Hspace) [ ForOpts | ForOptsBare ] ForVar KwIn "(" :whitespace(Hspace)
              ForFSrc ")" :whitespace(Hspace) KwDo Body ;
// The options may also stand unquoted when they hold no space: `for /f usebackq %%i`.
ForOpts     <~~ push({opts: up.in}) ~~> = '"' :whitespace() { NotDQ } '"' :whitespace(Hspace) ;
ForOptsBare <~~ push({opts: "\x22" + up.in + "\x22"}) ~~>
            = !"%%" BareChar :whitespace() { BareChar } :whitespace(Hspace) ;
ForFSrc     = ForFLit | ForFCmd | ForFBq | ForFFile ;
// The string runs to the LAST quote before the closing ), so it may hold quotes:
// `("f "a b" c")` is the text `f "a b" c`.
ForFLit     <~~ push({lit: makeWord(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = DqOpen :whitespace() { DPart | LitInnerDq } DqClose :whitespace(Hspace) ;
LitInnerDq  <~~ push(makeStr("\x22")) ~~> = '"' NotLitEnd ;
NotLitEnd   = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc == 32 || cc == 9) { i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i) }
        return cc == 41 ? abnf.newToken("\x01string-ends", 0) : undefined
    })()
~~) ;
// The single-quoted form is a COMMAND whose output is parsed. External programs are out of
// scope for this runtime, so the command is a chain of the internal commands that produce
// capturable output - echo and ver.
ForFCmd     <~~ push({cmd: pop()}) ~~> = "'" :whitespace(Hspace) SqChain "'" :whitespace(Hspace) ;
// Under the usebackq option the command is back-quoted instead (and a double-quoted
// set names a FILE - see makeForF).
ForFBq      <~~ push({cmd: pop(), bq: true}) ~~> = "`" :whitespace(Hspace) SqChain "`" :whitespace(Hspace) ;
SqChain     <~~ push(makeSeq(takeAll())) ~~> = SqCmd { "&" SqCmd } ;
SqCmd       = SqEchoDot | SqEcho | SqVer | SqExt ;
// Any other command there is one this runtime cannot run (see ExtCmd).
SqExt       <~~ push(makeExtCmd(takeAll(), up.start)) ~~> = SqName SqText ;
SqName      <~~ push(up.in) ~~> = SqNameCh :whitespace() { SqNameCh } :whitespace(Hspace) ;
SqNameCh    = "\x22"..."\x24" | "\x28"..."\x5f" | "\x61"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SqVer       <~~ push(makeVer()) ~~> = KwVer ;
// cmd parses the command inside for /f ('...') with the same command parser, so the glued
// `echo.` / `echo:` / `echo/` separator (see EchoDot) applies here too.
SqEchoDot   <~~ push(makeEcho(pop())) ~~> = KwEchoW :whitespace() EchoSepCh SqText ;
SqEcho      <~~ push(makeEcho(pop())) ~~> = KwEcho SqText ;
// Bare words are a FILE set. The path model behind this runtime answers existence, not
// contents, so a file set always reads nothing and the loop runs zero times.
ForFFile    <~~ var ws = takeAll(); push({file: ws[0]}) ~~> = Word { Word } ;
SqText      <~~ push(makeWord(takeAll())) ~~> = [ SqPart ] :whitespace() { SqPart } ;
SqPart      = ForRef | PctPct | VarPct | ParamStar | ParamRef | BangVar | PctLit | BangLit | SqChars ;
SqChars     <~~ push(makeStr(up.in)) ~~> = SqChar :whitespace() { SqChar } ;
For         <~~ push(makeFor(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwFor ForVar KwIn "(" :whitespace(Hspace) { Word | Newline } ")" :whitespace(Hspace) KwDo Body ;
// The set may span lines: `for %%a in ("f"` / `"g")`. for /d and for /r walk
// directories, which the path model cannot list (see the description): recognized
// and reported as not implemented.
ForDR       <~~ takeAll(); push(batUnsup("for /d and for /r (a directory walk)", up.start)) ~~>
            = KwFor ForDRSw [ !"%%" Word ] ForVar KwIn "(" :whitespace(Hspace) { Word | Newline } ")"
              :whitespace(Hspace) KwDo Body ;
ForDRSw     = ( "/d" | "/D" | "/r" | "/R" ) :whitespace(Hspace) ;
ForVar      = "%%" :whitespace() ForLetter :whitespace(Hspace) ;
ForNum      <~~ push(makeWord(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ForNumPart :whitespace() { ForNumPart } :whitespace(Hspace) ;
ForNumPart  = ForRef | VarPct | ParamRef | BangVar | ForNumChars ;
ForNumChars <~~ push(makeStr(up.in)) ~~> = FNChar :whitespace() { FNChar } ;
// A bound that is not a number counts as 0, as in set /a: `(a,2,3)` starts at 0.
FNChar      = "0"..."9" | "-" | "+" | "a"..."z" | "A"..."Z" ;
// A body is a ( ... ) block (statements separated by newlines) or a single statement.
Body        = Block | Single ;
Block       <~~ push({body: makeSeq(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = "(" :whitespace(Hspace) OptNL { Chain SepNL } ")" :whitespace(Hspace) ;
Single      <~~ push({body: pop()}) ~~> = Stmt ;
Group       <~~ push(makeGroup(pop())) ~~> = Block ;
// `a && (b) else c` - an else after a block that is no if body. What cmd.exe makes of
// it depends on where the chain splits (wine's suite records it), which this grammar
// does not model: reported as not implemented.
GroupElse   <~~ takeAll(); push(batUnsup("a ( ) block followed by else", up.start)) ~~>
            = Block KwElse Body ;
OptNL       = { Newline } ;
SepNL       = { Newline } ;

// ----- conditions -----
Cond        = ErrCond | ExistCond | DefinedCond | CmpCond | StrCond ;
// 'if errorlevel N' is true when the code is N or greater.
ErrCond     <~~ push(mkErrLevel(pop())) ~~> = KwErrorlevel Word ;
// `if exist` answers from the path model described in fsKind (no real filesystem).
ExistCond   <~~ push(mkExist(pop())) ~~> = KwExist Word ;
DefinedCond <~~ push(mkDefined(pop())) ~~> = KwDefined SetName ;
CmpCond     <~~ push(mkCmp(takeAll())) ~~> = CmpArg CmpOp CmpArg ;
StrCond     <~~ push(mkStrCmp(takeAll())) ~~> = CmpArg "==" <~~ push("==") ~~> CmpArg ;
// An operand ends at `==` even with no space around it: `if 1==1 echo` compares 1 and
// 1. A single = and the parentheses are ordinary text: `if (a)==(b) (`.
CmpArg      = QWord | CmpWord ;
CmpWord     <~~ push(makeWord(takeAll())) ~~>
            = CmpPart :whitespace() { CmpPart } :whitespace(Hspace) ;
CmpPart     = DQuote | ForRef | PctPct | VarPct | ParamStar | ParamRef | BangVar
            | EscNL | EscCh | PctLit | BangLit | CmpBare ;
CmpBare     <~~ push(makeStr(up.in)) ~~> = CmpCh :whitespace() { CmpCh } :whitespace() ;
CmpCh       = "\x23"..."\x24" | "\x27"..."\x29" | "\x2a"..."\x3b" | "=" NotEqNext | "\x3f"..."\x5d"
            | "\x5f"..."\x7b" | "\x7d"..."\x7e" | "\U000000a1"..."\U0010ffff" ;
NotEqNext   = :script(~~
